      "Why Subterranean Mycorrhizal Fungi Govern Forest Carbon Forecasts",
      "Global climate models have long represented temperate and boreal forests primarily from the ground up: orbiting satellites measure green leaf area, field foresters tally trunk diameters, and carbon-accounting protocols estimate how much atmospheric carbon dioxide trees lock into heartwood and fallen litter. Beneath every square meter of forest soil, however, kilometers of microscopic fungal threads—mycorrhizal hyphae—form symbiotic trading networks around tree roots. Over the past decade, soil biogeochemists have demonstrated that ignoring which guild of root fungi dominates a forest stand can throw fifty-year carbon projections off by wide margins.\n\nForest trees rarely absorb mineral nitrogen and phosphorus efficiently on their own; instead, they pipe ten to thirty percent of the liquid sugars fixed during photosynthesis underground to symbiotic fungi in exchange for scavenged soil nutrients and summer drought protection. Crucially, the two dominant fungal guilds operate contrasting chemical economies. Ectomycorrhizal fungi, commonly partnered with northern pines, oaks, and birches, secrete specialized enzymes that strip nitrogen directly from tough organic leaf litter, leaving nitrogen-poor carbon compounds behind that free-living decay microbes struggle to digest. Arbuscular mycorrhizal trees such as sugar maples, ashes, and tulip poplars, by contrast, promote faster microbial turnover and mineral-associated soil carbon under warmer, nitrogen-rich conditions. Shifting tree-fungal partnerships under acid deposition or climate warming therefore (23).\n\nMapping invisible underground symbioses across entire continents once seemed impossible because digging root cores every few kilometers was prohibitively labor-intensive. Quantitative ecologists are now pairing global crowd-sourced DNA metabarcoding databases of soil cores with airborne hyperspectral canopy imaging, since trees partnered with different fungal guilds display distinct foliar nitrogen signatures detectable from research aircraft and satellites. Incorporating fungal-guild maps into Earth-system simulations has already improved predictions of which northern forests buffer drought stress and retain topsoil carbon after timber harvests. (24), reforestation agencies are testing native soil inoculants when planting degraded strip-mined hillsides where beneficial fungi vanished decades ago. Managing forests as durable carbon sinks ultimately requires policymakers to (25).",
      "地球気候モデルは長らく温帯林や北方林を主に地上部から表現してきた。軌道衛星が緑葉面積を測り、林業家が幹の直径を集計し、炭素算定手順が樹木や落葉に固定される二酸化炭素量を推計してきたのである。しかし森林土壌1平方メートルごとの地下には何キロもの微細な菌糸――菌根菌のネットワーク――が樹木の根を取り巻く共生取引網を築いている。過去10年間で土壌生物地球化学者は、どのタイプの根圏共生菌が林分を優占しているかを無視すると50年先の森林炭素予測が大きく狂い得ることを実証した。樹木は単独では窒素やリンを効率よく吸収しにくく、光合成で作った糖の10〜30％を地下の共生菌へ送り、引き換えに土壌栄養塩と乾燥耐性を得ている。決定的なのは主要な2つの菌群が対照的な化学経済を営む点だ。マツ・ナラ・カバノキ類と組む外生菌根菌は特殊な酵素で難分解性の落葉から窒素を直接引き抜くため、残された貧窒素の炭素化合物は自由生活性の分解微生物が消化しにくく土壌に蓄積しやすい。対照的にカエデやトネリコ、ユリノキなどのアーバスキュラー菌根樹は温暖で窒素豊富な環境下で速い微生物回転と鉱物結合型炭素を促す。したがって酸性沈着や温暖化による樹木・菌類パートナーシップの変化は(23)のである。かつては大陸規模で不可視の地下共生を地図化するのは不可能に思われたが、土壌DNAメタバーコーディング国際データベースと航空ハイパースペクトル樹冠観測（共生菌群ごとに葉の窒素分光特性が異なる性質を利用）を組み合わせることで地球システムモデルの予測精度が向上した。(24)、植林機関は有益な菌が数十年前に消えた露天掘り跡地の斜面へ苗木を植える際、在来土壌菌の接種を試験している。森林を持続的な炭素吸収源として管理するには政策立案者が(25)ことが不可欠だ。",
